cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "Code for: Camera-Orientation Effects in Robotic Viewpoint Acquisition for Feed-Forward 3D
Reconstruction in Manufacturing Inspection Workcells"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: "2026-04-14"
url: "https://github.com/Adam-yes/robotic-viewpoint-acquisition-for-3d-reconstruction"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Adam-yes/robotic-viewpoint-acquisition-for-3d-reconstruction"
license: MIT
abstract: >
  Path Matters investigates how the camera trajectory a robot uses to scan
  an object affects the quality of AI-based 3D reconstruction.  The project
  evaluates five scanning patterns, two camera orientation strategies, three
  reconstruction models (VGGT, Fast3R, SAM3D), and a reinforcement learning
  agent trained in Isaac Lab to select optimal viewpoints for a UR5e robot
  with an eye-in-hand camera.  Key findings: camera orientation (not
  trajectory geometry) is the dominant factor in reconstruction quality, and
  proximity shaping rewards are essential for RL convergence.
keywords:
  - 3D reconstruction
  - robotic scanning
  - viewpoint selection
  - reinforcement learning
  - VGGT
  - Isaac Sim
  - Isaac Lab
  - UR5e
  - ICP alignment
  - point cloud
